Fixes #199118

## What Problem This Solves

A timed-out LanceDB recall can still start search when its embedding resolves late. Search can also give the native query time already spent preparing or refreshing the table, extending work beyond the recall deadline.

## Why This Change Was Made

Keep the existing deadline across both phase boundaries: stop before search when embedding completes too late, and deduct table preparation before dispatching the native query. A fully spent budget rejects before vector execution; a partial budget passes only the remaining whole milliseconds to LanceDB.

The outer timeout result, authority checks, native query cancellation and subsequent healthy reuse remain in place. This repairs the existing shared deadline rather than introducing another timer or a public setting.

## Evidence

The final regressions fail four ways on base: late embedding admits search both before and after timeout settlement, an expired pre-read budget still returns rows, and a partially spent budget reaches native execution unchanged. Twelve controls pass on that base.

With the fix, all 95 unique tests across eight affected/consumer suites pass. The native LanceDB fixture verifies no vector query dispatch after expiry, a 5,000 ms budget reduced to 3,499 ms after 1,500.25 ms elapsed, native cancellation and a later healthy query. Recall, explicit tools, automatic recall, provider lifecycle and external table refresh controls pass.

Executed on macOS arm64 with Node 26.12.1 and native LanceDB 0.41.0. Embedding completion and elapsed time are controlled; the database and vector queries are real. This is not a live-provider or Gateway session. Production/test typechecks, focused lint, formatting and line-cap checks pass.

```sh
node scripts/test-projects.mts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/recall-service.test.ts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/doctor-contract-api.test.ts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/index.test.ts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/auto-recall.test.ts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/auto-recall.deadline.test.ts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/provider-runtime.test.ts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/embeddings.lifecycle.test.ts \
  extensions/memory-lancedb/lancedb-store.staleness.test.ts
```

### Launched Gateway proof — November 15

I ran the built CLI entry point, `node openclaw.mjs gateway run`, on macOS arm64 / Node 26.12.1 with native LanceDB 0.41.0. The route was authenticated `POST /tools/invoke` → the registered `memory_recall` tool → `startMemoryRecall` → `MemoryDB.search` → native LanceDB. `ltm search` bypasses the shared recall owner, so I did not use it as deadline proof.

The unmodified baseline was [cfa40212f9c6](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/cfa40212f9c6ce442a36e6ad6b8df07eec9b6ae5); the candidate was [91ac9e7d292b](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/91ac9e7d292b3c3aec0ce859d3e594ec6796dece). Each tree had its own built Gateway, disposable profile and native store. I created a synthetic preference through `memory_store`, then reused the same process, agent, store and stored ID for all recalls within that tree.

The configured local OpenAI-compatible HTTP fixture returned `[1,0]`. An explicit test observer delayed delivery of that real HTTP response to the embedding caller by 16 seconds, allowing the real 15-second timeout to settle first. Another wrapper awaited real table initialization/refresh, then added 16 seconds or 1.5 seconds before preparation returned. The wrappers returned original values/errors, delegated native execution unchanged and restored on exit; no production file, clock, deadline decision or tool result was replaced. Both trees received the same stimuli.

Observed behavior:

- Late embedding: baseline entered search and native execution with `timeoutMs:0` after the caller timed out; candidate released the delayed embedding without entering search.
- Exhausted preparation: baseline dispatched a native query with the original 14,997 ms after more than 16 seconds of preparation and returned one native row; candidate rejected with `Query timeout` before native dispatch. Both HTTP calls correctly returned the existing unavailable result at about 15 seconds.
- Partial preparation: baseline forwarded 14,997 ms unchanged after 1,502 ms of preparation; candidate reduced 14,998 ms to 13,495 ms after 1,502 ms. Both native queries returned one row.
- Recovery: the immediate post-embedding call honored cooldown without another embedding request. After waiting 60.5 real seconds, recall returned the original memory. Recall after exhausted preparation and the final recall also returned that same stored ID, with no restart or reset.

Decisive original stdout records follow; only filesystem paths were sanitized. The `observations` arrays are the complete event sets for those requests, including the delayed continuation after the HTTP timeout. A missing native dispatch is supported by the active observer's healthy native-query controls before and after it.

