### Launched Gateway proof — November 15

I reproduced both paths through independently built, isolated Gateways on macOS arm64 with Node 26.12.1: base `cfa40212f9c6ce442a36e6ad6b8df07eec9b6ae5` and fix `6d2cc31cc3a7c8527d70fda92e42deb22e7f60fc`. Both trees remain clean; no production or upstream test changes were needed. The existing regression report above is separate from these new runtime runs.

The route was the real `openclaw.mjs` CLI → compiled Gateway → authenticated WebSocket `cron.add` → native source/credential authority → Streamable HTTP MCP Events discovery/subscription → signed verification and callbacks through a certificate-verified HTTPS callback-only proxy → real HMAC/parser → SQLite ingress → native Cron admission/history. Actual `/healthz`, `/readyz`, `/startupz` and authenticated `health` succeeded, with build identity confirmed by the server. The original MCP Events implementation remains the owner, including work coauthored by [steipete](https://github.com/steipete) in #165399.

The relevant owners are the [callback admission handler](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/6d2cc31cc3a7c8527d70fda92e42deb22e7f60fc/extensions/mcp-events/src/http.ts), [subscription lifecycle](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/6d2cc31cc3a7c8527d70fda92e42deb22e7f60fc/extensions/mcp-events/src/service.ts), and [native source/lease authority](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/6d2cc31cc3a7c8527d70fda92e42deb22e7f60fc/src/plugins/service-mcp-events.ts).

On base, `2028-02-30T00:00:00Z` became March 1, activated the subscription, and drove a real renewal request 43,295 ms later. The fix recorded `subscribe_failed`, kept the binding pending with **no `refreshBefore`**, and made its ordinary retry 1,008 ms later. This is rejection before adopting a malformed renewal deadline; retry scheduling remains intact. A valid leap-day end-of-day response activated the **same binding/store/profile**, and a subsequent real timer accepted the offset lease `2028-03-01T03:01:00+03:00`.

Correctly signed non-leap February 29 and April 31 callbacks received 200 on base and 400 on the fix. Valid leap-day, offset-crossing-midnight and `24:00:00` callbacks received 200 on both versions. Invalid HMAC received 401 on both. After renewal, a new healthy event completed in the same profile. Actual terminal Cron history contains six successful synthetic runs on base and four on the fix. After joining the Gateway and its workers, an exclusive SQLite inspection found the corresponding native ingress transfer receipts; the fix has no invalid-date or bad-HMAC event IDs. Completed ingress intentionally clears its payload, so timestamp preservation was checked in the actual outgoing downstream requests, not inferred from cleared rows.

**External inputs and limits:** MCP, DNS, HTTPS callback routing, credentials and downstream inference responses are explicit synthetic fixtures. No live model ran: the documented custom-provider route received fixed Responses SSE text, allowing real admission/history owners to finish. The callback origin's synthetic public DNS answer passes the unmodified product public-address check; the fixture alone routes its verified TLS connection locally. This does not prove public DNS/TLS reachability or a live MCP/provider service.

The lease owner bounds expiry before the changed parser. To reach the calendar guard causally, both runs use an explicit test epoch at `2028-02-29T23:59:00Z`, advancing at real rate. A preload offsets only the immediate wall-clock reads in compiled `gateway-scheduler` and `service-mcp-events`. `Date.parse`, calendar arithmetic, real timers/monotonic time, parsing, HMAC, authentication, persistence and scheduling implementations are unchanged. Filesystem, lock, database and credential clocks retain real time. The second external subscription response is held until the first status is captured, then released unchanged. These are declared test inputs/observations, not real-date or unmodified-clock claims. Initial readiness reported CPU pressure while remaining ready with no failing components; native config watching used the product's polling fallback. No performance claim.

Builds used the official `qaRuntime` profile with the supported sealed remote worker-deploy archive exclusion. This is a local runtime build, **not** a full release, declaration or UI build. Both decisive Gateway children exited 0 after joined SIGTERM cleanup; all reserved ports were free. Earlier setup attempts were preserved and corrected before these complete runs (home isolation, readiness log spelling, synthetic inference auth/MCP handshake, and the completed-ingress payload oracle).

